= main =
== introduction ==
Occasionally I feel anxious that I will die before being able to explain
[[semantic_holism]] sufficiently clearly to do this beautiful semantic principle
justice. Of course, any philosopher worth their salt would be delighted to have
good reasons for believing a principle that is at once so clear, profound,
elegant, and (not least important) true, and yet so widely disbelieved.  After
all one of the chief joys of philosophy is the license it gives for largely
unsuccessful attempts to foist one's opinions on more or less innocent passers
by. The field is thus apt to attract those who enjoy the the painless suffering
of being a lone voice in the wilderness, and I am no exception.

It would, therefore, be disingenuous to count the fact that [[semantic_holism]]
and the accompanying doctrine of indeterminacy of translation are both widely
held to be either false, self-contradictory or incoherent as a tribulation for
those, like myself, who espouse the doctrine. 

Let us begin by noting just how far fetched, it appears that any direct examples
of indeterminacy of translation will ever be produced in practice.

Let us begin, however, by emphasising just how far-fetched it seems  for
semantic holism implausible it seems that the doctrine of
confirmational holism (and thence semantic holism) and the under-determinacy of
translation is to have any direct and straightforward exemplars. Confirmation
holism is the doctrine that whole theories are the primary objects of
confimation (and infirmation) - and that some sentences (in perfectly good
linguistic standing) do not have confirmation (or infirmation) conditions at
all. If one holds that 

What does irritate somewhat, however, is that the very tools which critics use
to formulate their claims that semantic holism is incoherent or
self-contradictory, demonstrate the falsehood of their claims. For those tools
provide beautifully clear examples of semantic holism in action, and are
produced by means which exemplify the indeterminacy of translation. The
computers and programs that nearly all of us use to write our essays and
articles provide perfectly clear practical exemplars of both semantic and
confirmation holism, and thereby demonstrate the coherence of the very doctrines
that the critics use them to decry.

